This enhanced version adds support for external scripts and validates input types. It also allows customization of output content type.",79,,"['Here is the solution code for the problem as described above.

```python
def generate_html_head(style_objects, html_type='text/html', include_scripts=False):
    if not isinstance(style_objects, list) or not all(hasattr(obj, 'url') for obj in style_objects):
        raise ValueError(""style_objects must be a list of objects with 'url' attribute"")

    link_tags = []
    script_tags = []

    for i, obj in enumerate(style_objects):
        if not hasattr(obj, 'content') and include_scripts:
            raise ValueError(""Each script object must have 'content' attribute when including scripts"")

        link_tags.append(f'<link rel=""stylesheet"" href=""{obj.url}"" id=""style_{i}"" />')

        if include_scripts:
            script_tags.append(f'<script>{obj.content}</script>')

    html_content = '\n'.join(link_tags)

    if include_scripts:
        html_content += '\n'.join(script_tags)

    return f""<html><head>\n{html_content}\n</head><body><p>This is a test paragraph.</p></body></html>""
```

In the above code, we first check if style_objects is a list and all objects in it have 'url' attribute. If not, raise ValueError. Then for each object in style_objects, create link tag with url set to obj.url and unique id for each link tag based on their position in style_objects list (index). If include_scripts is True, we also check if each script object has 'content' attribute, if not raise ValueError. For each script object, create a script tag using its content. Then concatenate all generated tags into html_content string. In the end, return html document with head containing html_content string.
