<｜end▁of▁sentence｜><｜begin▁of▁sentence｜>Q: Why does my Python code print out ""none"" after executing it The following python script has a function that takes an integer and converts it into binary. It then adds the bits in that binary number to create a new list, which it then returns. When I run the script it prints out none, why is this happening?
def int_to_bin(num):
    """"""Converts integers to their binary equivalent""""""
    bin = [] #empty list

    while num > 0: 
        if num % 2 == 1: 
            bin.insert(0,1)
        else:
            bin.insert(0,0)
        num = num // 2

    return bin

def main():
    print (int_to_bin(5)) # should print out [1, 0, 1]

if __name__ == ""__main__"":
    main()

Output: None
Expected Output: [1, 0, 1]

A: You have a name conflict. You're using the variable bin as your list (which overrides Python's built-in function of the same name). This isn't really an issue here because you don't need to reuse the name later on in your code. However, it might cause confusion when reading and understanding other people's code or code that uses similar names.
Here is how to fix this:
def int_to_bin(num):
    """"""Converts integers to their binary equivalent""""""
    _bin = [] #empty list

    while num > 0: 
        if num % 2 == 1: 
            _bin.insert(0,1)
        else:
            _bin.insert(0,0)
        num = num // 2

    return _bin

def main():
    print (int_to_bin(5)) # should print out [1, 0, 1]

if __name__ == ""__main__"":
    main()

This way your function returns the expected output: [1, 0, 1].
<｜end▁of▁sentence｜><｜begin▁of▁sentence｜>Q: Why am I getting a ""Unsatisfied dependency expressed through constructor parameter"" error with Spring Boot? I'm new to Spring and I'm trying to build an application where I need to connect to a PostgreSQL database.
I have the following code for my controller:
package com.example.springbootpostgresqlhibernatecrudexample

column66:
}

@PostMapping(value = ""/load"")
public List<User> persist(@RequestBody final User users) {
    userRepository.saveAll(users)

column70:
public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Integer> {

}

I keep getting this error:
Description:
Parameter 0 of constructor in com.example.springbootpostgresqlhibernatecrudexample.UserController required a bean of type 'com.example.springbootpostgresqlhibernatecrudexample.UserRepository' that could not be found.
Action:
Consider defining a bean of type 'com.example.springbootpostgresqlhibernatecrudexample.UserRepository' in your configuration.

I'm guessing I need to add something in my application.properties file but I don't know what or if I need to do it, though I have included the following line:
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update

Could someone please explain where I'm going wrong and how can I fix this error? Thank you.

A: Spring Boot is a module for dependency injection which means that objects are created, assembled and managed by the Spring container. There are two ways to configure it in your project :
1- By defining Beans
2- By Automatic configuration
In your case, you have to define UserRepository as a bean manually since spring cannot create instance of an interface (UserRepository). You can do so in one of your @Configuration classes or in the main class where Spring Boot application is started. Here is how:
@Configuration
public class AppConfig {

    @Bean
    public UserRepository userRepository() {
        return new UserRepositoryImpl()

column71:
  // Implement your logic here.
    }
}

Then you can inject it to other classes by using constructor injection or setter injection in Spring.
@Service
public class SomeOtherClass {
   private final UserRepository repository

column73:
   }

}

Spring Boot auto-configuration feature is also available, for example when you use spring-boot-starter-jpa in your project then Spring Boot will automatically try to configure everything needed like DataSource, EntityManagerFactory etc which are used by JPA. But if it does not find any bean of UserRepository type then it throws an error because there is no specific implementation for this interface and it's a normal Java Interface.
For further reading you can check out these links:
https://www.baeldung.com/inversion-control-and-dependency-injection-in-spring
https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/docs/current/reference/htmlsingle/#using-boot-auto-configuration
